---

<App title="Publishing guide · Plugins · Zeron" description="How to put a plugin in Zeron's store: what it needs, how to send it, and how Zeron verifies it." canonical="/plugins/publishing" current="guide" measure="prose">
  <h1 slot="title" class="t-title">Publishing guide</h1>

  <article class="guide prose">
    <p class="guide-lead">Anyone can share a Zeron plugin as a file or a link. The store is for the ones you want people to find: a version that passes the store's automatic checks is listed and installable the moment you send it, and Zeron verifies it once a reviewer has read it. Zeron says which versions are verified wherever it offers one.</p>

    <nav class="guide-parts" aria-label="On this page">
      <ol>
        {PARTS.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="needs">What the store needs</h2>
    <p>A plugin is a folder with its <code>zeron-plugin.json</code>. For the store, it also needs:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong><code>README.md</code></strong>, its page, at most 512 KB, in the store's Markdown (below). Pictures are files in the plugin, by a relative path (<code>![Board](shots/board.png)</code>). A picture from elsewhere shows as a link, because what is shown is what was reviewed.</li>
      <li><strong><code>description</code></strong>: one line, at most 300 characters, its summary everywhere it's listed. Its <code>name</code> is at most 64. Neither may claim Zeron's mark: the words “verified” or “official” in any spelling that reads as them (look-alike letters, letters spaced apart) or in the store's other languages (“oficial”, “officiel”, “offiziell”, “官方”, “公式”, “공식”…), or a check mark; nor hold a character drawn as nothing, but an emoji's own variation selector. Saying the plugin is Zeron's own is a warning in <code>zeron plugin check</code>, <code>claims_zeron</code>, and the store refuses it from anyone but Zeron: a name may say “Zeron” only as what the plugin is for (“Themes for Zeron”, “Git in Zeron”; never “Zeron Git” or “Zeron Labs”), and a description may say what it does with Zeron but not that it's by or from Zeron, nor Zeron's team, staff or approval.</li>
      <li><strong><code>icon</code></strong>: an SVG in the plugin, at most 32 KB. It's drawn in the text's colour, as Zeron draws its own.</li>
      <li><strong><code>categories</code></strong>: one or two of {CATEGORIES.map((c, i) => <>{i > 0 && (i === CATEGORIES.length - 1 ? " and " : ", ")}<code>{c.id}</code></>)}.</li>
      <li><strong><code>version</code></strong>: strict semver, <code>1.2.0</code> or <code>2.0.0-beta.1</code>. No <code>v</code>, no leading zeros.</li>
      <li><strong>A username</strong> on your Zeron account, so the store can say “by @username”. Zeron asks for one after you sign in.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The first account to publish an id keeps it. An account can take up to 5 new ids in any 7 days. Zeron's own plugins' ids, ids starting with <code>zeron</code>, and names that look like a brand's are held back.</p>
    <p>The rest is optional:</p>
    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`{
  "keywords": ["focus", "timer"],
  "homepage": "https://example.com/focus-timer",
  "repository": "https://github.com/you/focus-timer",
  "license": "MIT",
  "screenshots": ["shots/board.png", "shots/settings.png"],
  "engines": { "zeron": ">=0.4.0" }
}`}</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li><code>keywords</code>: up to 8, lowercase, at most 32 characters each.</li>
      <li><code>screenshots</code>: up to 8 PNG or JPEG pictures in the plugin, shown on its page.</li>
      <li><code>engines.zeron</code>: the Zeron versions it works with. Anyone on another can't install it, and its page says why.</li>
      <li><strong><code>CHANGELOG.md</code></strong> gives each version's notes: the section under the heading that starts with that version (<code>## 1.2.0</code>). People read them when Zeron offers the update.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 id="markdown">The store's Markdown</h3>
    <p>A README and a version's notes are drawn from this part of Markdown, on the web as in Zeron:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Headings (<code>#</code> to <code>######</code>, or underlined), paragraphs and line breaks.</li>
      <li><em>Emphasis</em>, <strong>strong</strong>, <del>strikethrough</del> (<code>~~</code>), and <code>`code`</code>.</li>
      <li>Code blocks, fenced (with a language, to highlight it) or indented.</li>
      <li>Quotes, numbered and bulleted lists, and task lists (<code>- [ ]</code>, <code>- [x]</code>), nested up to 32 deep.</li>
      <li>Tables, up to 64 columns.</li>
      <li>Rules (<code>---</code>).</li>
      <li>Links: inline, by reference, and <code>&lt;https://…&gt;</code> or a bare <code>https://</code> address. A link goes only to https or mail; a link to a file of the plugin shows as its words.</li>
      <li>Pictures, as above, also inside a link (a badge).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Anything else is shown as you typed it, characters and all: raw HTML (<code>&lt;p align="center"&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;br&gt;</code>) and footnotes (<code>[^1]</code>) included. Math (<code>$…$</code>, a <code>$$</code> block, or a fence marked <code>math</code>) is drawn in Zeron but shown as typed here. <code>zeron plugin check</code> says where a README or CHANGELOG has HTML, a footnote or display math, as a note, not a failure. Use Markdown for pictures and headings. Nothing in a README runs.</p>
    <p>The package, as sent, is at most 20 MB. Files that aren't text or pictures, a compiled program say, are allowed, but reviewers look closely at them.</p>

    <h2 id="send">Sending a version</h2>
    <p>Check it first: <code>zeron plugin check &lt;folder&gt;</code> lists everything the store would refuse, before anything is sent. It needs no Zeron running, so a pull request's CI can run it too.</p>
    <p>Then send it, from Zeron or a terminal:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>In Zeron: <strong>Settings</strong> › <strong>Plugins</strong> › <strong>Yours</strong> › <strong>Publish…</strong></li>
      <li>From a terminal, with Zeron running and signed in: <code>zeron plugin publish &lt;id&gt;</code>. <code>--dry-run</code> checks it without sending it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The plugin is signed with your device's key and sent with your account. <a href="/account">Your plugins</a> shows each version's state, and a reviewer's words when there are some.</p>

    <h2 id="review">Verification</h2>
    <p>Every version is listed once it passes the automatic checks: the package, its signature, its size, its version, new programs and hosts. A reviewer then reads it. They look at what it asks for (what's new since your last verified version first, and anything that reaches past Zeron most closely), what changed in its files, and its page as visitors will see it.</p>
    <p>Zeron verifies it, or doesn't, with one of these reasons and the reviewer's note:</p>
    <ul>
      {DECLINE_REASONS.map((r) => <li>{r.label}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>A version that isn't verified stays listed: everyone sees only that it isn't verified yet, and installing it always asks first. You read the reason and the reviewer's note in Zeron, the command line and <a href="/account">Your plugins</a>; send the fix as a new version. Say plainly in the README what your plugin runs and where it connects; it's what a reviewer asks for most.</p>
    <p>Anyone signed in can report a listed plugin. The reviewers can take a harmful version down, which turns it off on the devices that have it, or remove one that shouldn't be in the store; its author reads why.</p>

    <h2 id="versions">Versions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Each version is sent once and keeps its content for good. Each must be later than every version you sent before.</li>
      <li>Every verified version can be installed, and so can the newest; each says whether it's verified.</li>
      <li>A version that isn't verified stops taking new installs once a newer one is published: a release replaces older releases and pre-releases, a pre-release older pre-releases only. It stays so unless a reviewer verifies it later. Copies already installed keep working.</li>
      <li>A pre-release (<code>2.0.0-beta.1</code>) is offered only to people who chose that plugin's pre-releases.</li>
      <li><strong>Stop new installs</strong> of a version (those who have it keep it working), and allow them again, except for a version a newer one replaced. From Your plugins, Zeron, or <code>zeron plugin yank|unyank &lt;id&gt; &lt;version&gt;</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="ci">Publishing from CI</h2>
    <p>A <strong>deploy key</strong> lets a CI job, such as a GitHub Action, send new versions without your session. A key is your account's, made in Zeron on your machine, with a scope you choose then and can't change: the plugins it names, or all of yours, those you publish later included. A plugin's first version always comes from your machine; a key sends the ones after it.</p>
    <p>Make one in Zeron, or from a terminal with Zeron running and signed in:</p>
    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`zeron plugin key create --name "GitHub Actions" --plugins focus-timer,focus-board
zeron plugin key create --name "All my plugins" --all
zeron plugin key create --name "Release CI" --plugins focus-timer --expires-in-days 90
zeron plugin key list
zeron plugin key revoke <key>`}</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li><code>key create</code> asks you to sign in again first. <code>--name</code> says what it's for. Its scope is <code>--plugins a,b</code> (plugins you already have in the store) or <code>--all</code> (every plugin of yours, those you first publish later included, never one transferred to you), fixed once made: another set of plugins is another key. <code>--expires-in-days</code> stops it working after that many days.</li>
      <li>Its secret (<code>zpk_…</code>) is printed once, and Zeron keeps no copy.</li>
      <li><code>key list</code> shows your live keys: each one's name, what it publishes, when it was made and last used, and when it ends; never their secrets. So does <a href="/account/keys">Deploy keys</a>.</li>
      <li><code>key revoke</code> takes the key as <code>key list</code> shows it. It publishes nothing from then on; what it published stays.</li>
      <li>An account has at most 20 live keys, and makes at most 10 a day.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Put the secret in your CI's secrets as <code>ZERON_DEPLOY_KEY</code>, then publish with it. It's read from the environment only, never from an argument, and needs no Zeron running and nobody signed in:</p>
    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`ZERON_DEPLOY_KEY=zpk_… zeron plugin publish <folder>`}</code></pre>
    <p>It exits 0 when published, 1 when the plugin doesn't meet the store's rules, 2 for a set-up problem, 3 when the store refused the key or the account, and 4 when the store couldn't be reached.</p>
    <p>A key sends new versions of the plugins in its scope, nothing else, and each is listed as any version is, not yet verified; your devices tell you when a key publishes one. A version it sends is installable at once, so treat a key like a password: if one leaks, revoke it (from <a href="/account/keys">Deploy keys</a>, Zeron, or <code>zeron plugin key revoke</code>) and stop new installs of what it sent.</p>
    <p>On GitHub, the publish action installs Zeron's command line and sends the plugin with your key:</p>
    <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`- uses: actions/checkout@v4
- uses: zeronsh/zeron/actions/publish-plugin@v0.4.0
  with:
    key: \${{ secrets.ZERON_DEPLOY_KEY }}`}</code></pre>
    <p>Pin it to a Zeron release tag or a commit, never a branch: the step holds your key. Without a key (a pull request from a fork gets no secrets) it only checks.</p>

    <p class="guide-next"><a class="btn secondary sm" href="/account"><Glyph name="folder-with-files" size={14} />Your plugins</a></p>
  </article>
</App>
